  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSoutheasterly flow will strengthen this afternoon as the remnant\ncirculation of Isaias approaches from the south, establishing a\n40-55KT low-level jet (a decrease from the near 70KT forecast\nfrom yesterday). Forecast profiles indicate a narrow stable \nlayer that will contain much of this momentum, with only a \nmodest isentropic downglide signal off the higher terrain. A \nsignificant fraction of the jet could still mix to the surface \nin spots. Gusts of 30 to 50 mph are expected in the lee of the \nhigher terrain and on higher ridgetops beginning this afternoon \nand continuing into midday Sunday. The existing Wind Advisory \nwill continue for these areas. Did remove the mention of \nisolated gusts to 60 mph in the wind advisory given lower \nforecast momentum aloft.\n\nAcross the remainder of the forecast area, gusts of 15 to 30 \nmph are possible late this afternoon into Sunday morning as the \nboundary layer slowly saturates with precipitation and allows \nlimited mixing of the strong low level wind field. High-rooted \nelevated convection overnight atop the stable layer could \noccasionally mix stronger winds to the surface, though a \nsubstantial saturated layer beneath any updrafts should limit \nthis potential. Winds should taper through the day Sunday as \nhigher momentum air aloft shifts northeast. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe heaviest widespread rainfall rates will develop late \ntonight into Sunday afternoon within a tropical moisture plume, \nsupported by modest but deep instability and deep warm-cloud \ndepths. Heaviest precipitation will generally focus on the \neastern slopes of the higher terrain via upslope enhancement and\nacross parts of the Middle Ohio Valley Sunday morning in outer\nbands banded precipitation. Most locations can expect between \none half and two inches of rainfall. Lesser amounts are possible\nin strong downslope regions, while locally higher totals remain\npossible in persistent banding or upslope. Antecedent \nconditions are dry, and soils should absorb the majority of this\nprimarily beneficial rainfall without widespread issues. The\ncombination of a long stretch of dry and relatively calm weather\nhas likely left a decent number of weakened trees ready to fall\nwithout much provocation. Rain loading coupled with gusty winds\nwill likely cause some tree fall, even outside of the higher\nwind gusts.\n\nThe primary concern for any flash flooding centers on any slow-\nmoving banded convection nearer the remnant circulation core \nSunday afternoon. Convective rates could exceed 2 inches per \nhour in a very localized fashion and linger over isolated spots \nfor a couple of hours. A somewhat persistent signal for this has\nappeared in recent CAMs, though placement remains inconsistent \nwith uncertainty in the track of the low. A short-fused, \nnarrowly focused flash flood watch could be considered late \novernight or Sunday morning if these trends continue, but \nconfidence at this juncture is too low given uncertainty in both\nlow placement and antecedent rainfall totals. Outside this \ncorridor, only isolated, mainly nuisance high water issues are \npossible where heavier rates coincide with a narrow hollow, poor\ndrainage, or leaf-clogged drains. Rain will taper off Sunday \nnight into Monday morning as the weakening system lifts toward \nthe Atlantic. High pressure then returns briefly, with another \nweak cold front expected Tuesday night. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nA narrow temporal window for decent surface heating is expected\nSunday afternoon ahead of the eastern flank of the remnant low-\nlevel circulation, where a dry slot is expected to manifest. \nNear surface-based parcel ascent remains possible in a mixed- \nlayer environment conducive to rotating showers or \nthunderstorms. There could be some low level curvature beneath \nany of these cores and couldn't rule out a tornado. Confidence \nis low given the narrow window for this to occur and dependence \non the exact track and intensity of the remnant circulation (of \nwhich there is still a decent spread in the CAMs), but the setup\ncontinues warrant monitoring. At this time SPC maintains only a\ngeneral risk of thunder.",
